    "created_at": "2017-08-08T23:44:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-08-08T23:44:35Z",
    "body": "> I don't understand, @overholt pasted a link above suggesting Apple has very similar restrictions.\r\n\r\nThanks, I'm so sorry I missed that. OK, so that's a positive sign for multi-implementer interest in some restriction. Although I am still scared that we won't be able to get good interop on the actual restriction.\r\n\r\nTo quote the Apple documentation:\r\n\r\n> To prevent users from maliciously redirecting phone calls or changing the behavior of a phone or account, the Phone app supports most, but not all, of the special characters in the tel scheme. Specifically, if a URL contains the `*` or `#` characters, the Phone app does not attempt to dial the corresponding phone number.\r\n\r\nI don't have an iPhone to test, but my interpretation of this is that it will launch the Phone app, and just not auto-dial. Are we interested in such an implementation in Chrome and Firefox? Note also it's the `*` and `#` characters *anywhere* in the string.\r\n\r\nThat said, I still am unclear whether such a guard is necessary, as per recent discussions in this thread. Especially if it's necessary on older Android versions and not newer ones, that definitely doesn't seem like material we should include in the HTML spec...",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
